WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.139 --> 00:00:04.290
Hey guys…it’s day 25 of quarantining in
our house.

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:09.930
Like you, we’re binge-watching Tiger King,
stress baking, and (less fun) experiencing

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:12.170
a reduction in our income.

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:17.060
We realize we’re in an incredibly fortunate
position; being able to ride it out without

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:21.509
making significant sacrifices, but that is
not the case for most.

00:00:21.509 --> 00:00:29.609
As we’ve mentioned before, 40% of Americans
can’t pay for a $400 emergency without credit.

00:00:29.609 --> 00:00:34.950
Even if you’re not facing a corona-induced-cash-crisis
YET, that doesn’t mean you’re out of the

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:35.950
woods.

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:40.239
The economic fallout from this disaster IS
going to have an effect on your life, no matter

00:00:40.239 --> 00:00:42.289
what industry you’re in.

00:00:42.289 --> 00:00:46.329
Unfortunately, like this virus, anxiety is
also contagious.

00:00:46.329 --> 00:00:51.590
So it’s only natural that a lot of us are
asking ourselves the same question: How do

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:55.440
I survive a financial storm?

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:59.359
Your first step needs to be to create an Emergency
Budget.

00:00:59.359 --> 00:01:00.359
Yep.

00:01:00.359 --> 00:01:04.559
If you haven’t yet climbed on board the
Two Cents budget train, 2020 is the perfect

00:01:04.559 --> 00:01:05.990
time to start.

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:11.470
First rule of emergency budgeting is it must
be written or typed out -- not in your head.

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:14.829
So find a spreadsheet or sign up for a budgeting
software.

00:01:14.829 --> 00:01:16.420
Some are completely free.

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:18.990
Second step is to gather data from the past.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:23.780
That way you’re working with real numbers
and not the amount you THINK you spend.

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:27.810
Start by looking at the last 2 months worth
of transactions and make a list of everything

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:29.400
you spend money on.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:34.820
This might take a little time, but hey, for
a lot of us, time seems more plentiful than

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:35.820
ever.

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:39.329
Start by grouping the essential expenses at
the top.

00:01:39.329 --> 00:01:43.759
Housing, utilities, basic groceries, transportation
and medical needs.

00:01:43.759 --> 00:01:48.720
Second, list debt payments, like student loans
or credit card minimums.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:53.120
Finally list all your discretionary expenses,
like entertainment, meal delivery,

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:54.380
and subscriptions.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:57.180
What you’re looking at is your pre-crisis
budget.

00:01:57.189 --> 00:02:01.270
Now it’s time to use that data to draft
your emergency budget.

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:06.039
Your first draft should take on a “Scorched
Earth” feel by cutting everything below

00:02:06.039 --> 00:02:07.890
your minimum debt payments.

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:11.260
Spotify, wine, Postmates, even Netflix.

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:12.310
Feels pretty bleak, right?

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:14.010
Don’t worry, we’re not done.

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:19.150
Now it’s time to create a second draft where
you strategically pick discretionary items

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:20.160
to add back in.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:23.650
This might be just one thing, like Netflix.

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:28.290
Or if your income took a less drastic hit,
you can add back 3 or 4 things.

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:33.140
It might come as a surprise, but holding onto
a couple tiny splurge items might actually

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:38.350
help you more in the long run than cutting
them all completely.One survey showed over

00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:43.920
half of americans owned up to impulsively
shopping as a result of feelings of anxiety,

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:49.409
stress and depression and 83% of those stress
spenders regret those purchases later.

00:02:49.409 --> 00:02:53.849
For example, I find a lot of relief in being
able to play a few online games.

00:02:53.849 --> 00:02:58.780
So I’d rather maintain access to that outlet
and cut out ordering in because I find cooking

00:02:58.780 --> 00:02:59.879
less stressful.

00:02:59.879 --> 00:03:01.240
You might have a different preference.

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:06.190
The point is that you need to give the pressure
cooker a small but intentional release, that

00:03:06.190 --> 00:03:09.390
way you’re less likely to make a massive
impulse buy later.

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:13.590
Now that most of your discretionary spending
is paused, it’s time to try and slim down

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:17.690
the expenses that make up the majority of
your needs, your essentials.

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:23.720
And with almost 7 million applying for unemployment
just last week, lenders and utility companies

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:25.340
are being especially lenient.

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:29.840
If you own a home with a mortgage and have
had your income go down, reach out to your

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:33.210
mortgage company immediately to discuss options.

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:37.920
The two mortgage giants Fannie Mae and Freddie
Mac are rolling out relief measures to help

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:42.580
millions of homeowners defer or re-work their
loan payments.

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:47.340
Even institutions like Chase, Bank of America,
and Wells Fargo all are coming out with their

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:48.739
own offerings.

00:03:48.739 --> 00:03:52.959
And while landlords are less regulated than
mortgage lenders, they also might be able

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:58.760
to extend you more time or be a little more
flexible on terms, but again, you need to ask.

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:03.299
Your next round of calls should be to your
utility companies &amp; insurance providers.

00:04:03.299 --> 00:04:08.010
Many are offering to give their customers
leeway to skip or delay payments.

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:12.230
As you work your way down your list of bills,
you’ll finally get to your minimum loan

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:13.230
payments.

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:17.420
Thankfully, all federal student loan payments
have been suspended by the recent stimulus

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:22.830
bill until september 30 and even private lenders
are offering forbearance programs.

00:04:22.830 --> 00:04:26.730
You should also reach out to your credit card
companies and any other lenders on your list.

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:30.010
They’re all joining the “less people
can pay us” party.

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:34.630
The one thing that most of these forms of
relief have in common is that benefits are

00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:36.220
not automatic.

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:39.640
You have to reach out and ask them for help.

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:41.760
Proactivity is the name of the game.

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:46.030
And even if they don’t let you skip a payment,
they might have the power to not let it affect

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:47.100
your credit score.

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:48.690
That alone would be worth the call.

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:53.660
And if you already have cash set aside, or
are expecting some in the form of, say, a

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:58.330
stimulus check from Uncle Sam you should keep
that on hand to cover your expenses your income

00:04:58.330 --> 00:04:59.410
can’t.

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:03.800
While it might be tempting to pay off debt
or even invest with the recent stock-market

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:07.200
drop, cash is king in a crisis.

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:12.480
Even if your income is only somewhat hit,
it’s best use is to plump up that emergency

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:13.950
fund a bit more.

00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:16.360
Just in case Murphy’s law decides to show
up.

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:21.890
And even with flexible payments and forbearance
plans coming online, we see many folks considering

00:05:21.890 --> 00:05:23.620
extreme measures.

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:29.730
Like taking on 5 figures of credit card debt,
yanking from retirement or even ignoring bills

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:31.170
entirely.

00:05:31.170 --> 00:05:34.430
Is there a time and a place to take these
drastic steps?

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:35.430
Maybe.

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:38.040
But they should be an absolute last resort.

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:42.130
We definitely don’t recommend taking out
credit cards or loans if you have cash you

00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:45.330
can access: that’s exactly what it’s for!

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:49.330
You truly need to be living the “scorched
earth” lifestyle in earnest before resorting

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:50.660
to those tools.

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:55.410
And if you’re in survival mode and debt
is not an option, prioritize paying for your

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:59.770
basic food, utilities, and transportation
you’d need to keep yourself employable.

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:05.000
If you still have leftover funds, move on
to your housing costs, and minimum debt payments.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:10.090
The main reason putting your utilities before
your housing costs makes sense is that the

00:06:10.090 --> 00:06:13.580
consequences set in more quickly than skipping
a housing payment.

00:06:13.580 --> 00:06:15.530
There’s no way to sugar-coat it.

00:06:15.530 --> 00:06:17.490
It’s an especially scary time.

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:20.690
But it doesn’t have to be an unmitigated
disaster.

00:06:20.690 --> 00:06:24.650
Don’t allow panic or shame to take complete
control.

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:28.650
You have the power to make a plan and reach
out for help.

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:29.660
